sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R2-R4 and 5, single-swamp-node + light-boxes-use-swamp-ssh, node-wireguard-change-keeps-public-ssh. docs/reference/wireguard-endpoints.md.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02 pass 2, rule 22 (Q-WGHUB: files from the Gateway, key composed on the Node; no snapshot or restore). Hub peers are one file each (race found 2026-11-02).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: node:network:converge
  %% kind: node.network_converge
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Node 12<br/>WireGuard peer
  participant H as vpn Node 1<br/>WireGuard hub
  CLI->>GW: node:network:converge 12<br/>--idempotency-key=KEY
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit node:network:converge 12`
  %%> - syntax: `<node> [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Node, by ID or name; no keys, scripts or placement options
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - brings Node 12's WireGuard peer, resolver and peer trust in line with its saved network policy
  %%> - publishes the current peer list on the hub
  Note over GW: Validate, capture policy<br/>task #5170 · Node + hub lock
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12
  %%> - Node 12 is an active, managed Linux Node with a WireGuard address and a public SSH address
  %%> - no other network change runs for Node 12 or the hub; the Gateway holds one lock for each and waits briefly for a busy one
  %%> Builds:
  %%> - the peer config without a private key: address, hub endpoint and public key, DNS server and domains
  %%> - the hub peer list, and the WireGuard trust rule for Node 12
  %%> Result:
  %%> - task #5170 recorded with the full intent
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-network
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the peer config, the hub peer list and the resolver policy
  %%> - the addresses and SSH host keys of Node 12 and Node 1; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5170
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  SW->>N: Prepare the key<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · wg genkey
  %%> How:
  %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with `@swamp/ssh`
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - creates `/etc/wireguard/orbit.key` (0600) only when none exists, and prints the public key; the private key never leaves Node 12
  Note over SW: Check the key<br/>typed check
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - refuses a key that differs from the stored one or collides with another Node's key
  SW->>H: Write the hub peer file<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · 0600
  %%> How:
  %%> - over SSH from 209; Node 1 has no Swamp binary and no worker
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes only this Node's `/etc/wireguard/orbit-peers.d/12.conf` on the hub
  %%> Why one file per peer:
  %%> - a whole-file render races: two Nodes joining at once would each load a list without the other and drop it
  SW->>H: Load the hub peer<br/>wg addconf
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - adds the peer live; every other peer and the hub's interface stay as they are, and the hub does not restart
  opt the stored key changed
    SW->>H: Drop the old key<br/>wg set … remove
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - removes the Node's old key from the live hub
  end
  SW->>N: Write the peer config<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · 0600
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes the Gateway-rendered `orbit.conf`; its `PostUp` reads the key file, so the file holds no secret
  SW->>N: Write the peer list<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · 0600
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes the hub `[Peer]` section alone, for `wg syncconf`
  SW->>N: Start the tunnel<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/systemd
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - keeps `wg-quick@orbit` enabled and running; it starts it only when it is down
  SW->>N: Load the peer<br/>wg syncconf · key composed on Node
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - loads the peer list into the running tunnel, composing `PrivateKey` from the key file on the Node; no restart
  SW->>N: Trust WireGuard peers<br/>ufw allow · one argv
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - adds Orbit's managed ufw rule that trusts the WireGuard member range; an identical rule is left as it is
  SW->>N: Read the DNS policy<br/>wg show · resolvectl
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one read command prints the public key, resolver servers, `orbit` domains and default route, the DNS marker and the hook line
  Note over SW: Decide the DNS change<br/>typed check
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - compares the read with the admitted policy; a match skips the DNS writes below
  %%> - refuses a live key that is not the prepared one
  SW->>N: Keep the DNS hook<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/line
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - keeps the Gateway-rendered `PostUp = resolvectl …` line in `orbit.conf`, so the policy returns when the tunnel starts
  %%> - leaves every other line of the file as it is
  SW->>N: Write the DNS marker<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · 0600
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - records the link, server and domains in `/etc/wireguard/orbit.dns-link`
  SW->>N: Set the DNS policy<br/>resolvectl dns · domain · route
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one command sets the server and routing domains on `orbit` with `default-route no`; only Orbit names go to VPN DNS
  opt policy was on another link
    SW->>N: Clear the old link<br/>dns · domains · networkctl
    %%> When:
    %%> - only when an earlier release put the policy on another link
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - one command clears the old server and domains there, gives the uplink its default route back and reconfigures it
  end
  SW->>N: Read the DNS policy again<br/>wg show · resolvectl
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the same read as before, for the end check
  Note over SW: Check the network<br/>typed check
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - requires the admitted DNS policy, the prepared key live, and a usable uplink unless the Node is an operator
  alt the check passes
    N-->>SW: tunnel and DNS ok
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the public key and the applied resolver policy
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record the result<br/>public key · task succeeded
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Node 12's public key and network policy are recorded as applied
    %%> - task #5170 succeeds
  else a step or the check fails
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or no handshake
    %%> When:
    %%> - a write, apply, firewall or resolver step fails, or the check finds no handshake or no DNS answer
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed, its error code, and whether the restore passed
    Note over GW: Mark the network failed<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task #5170 fails; the recorded public key stays the old one
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - a new key pair stays on Node 12 and is reused by a rerun; the firewall rule stays, which is harmless
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new task
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5170
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
